<h2>What Joseph Shine actually decided</h2>
<p>Section 497 IPC criminalised adultery in a form that treated the wife as her husband\'s property: only a man could be the offender, only a husband could complain, and a husband\'s consent to the act removed the offence altogether. The Constitution Bench — Chief Justice Dipak Misra with Justices Nariman, Khanwilkar, Chandrachud and Malhotra — struck the provision down as violative of Articles 14, 15 and 21, overruling the earlier decisions that had sustained it. Section 198(2) CrPC, the complaint gateway, fell with it. The judgment is a landmark of dignity and equality jurisprudence; what it is not, and never claimed to be, is a charter of immunity within marriage.</p>

<h2>The civil consequences that survive</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Divorce.</strong> Section 13(1)(i) HMA: a single act of voluntary post-marriage intercourse with another person grounds dissolution. Parallel provisions exist in the Divorce Act, 1869, the Special Marriage Act, 1954 and other personal laws.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Judicial separation.</strong> The same fact supports a decree of judicial separation under Section 10 HMA for a spouse not ready for final dissolution.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Maintenance.</strong> Section 144(4) BNSS disentitles a wife living in adultery; conduct also enters the discretionary alimony assessment under Section 25 HMA.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Cruelty overlap.</strong> A continuing adulterous relationship, openly carried on, is frequently pleaded as mental cruelty as well — the two grounds are commonly combined.</div>
</div>

<h2>Proof: inclination and opportunity</h2>
<p>Matrimonial courts decide these cases on preponderance of probabilities, not proof beyond reasonable doubt, and they have always accepted that adultery is proved circumstantially. The classical formulation looks for evidence of mutual inclination coupled with opportunity — sustained private association, shared accommodation, travel bookings, photographs and, in the modern docket, call detail records, chats and location data. Electronic evidence must be proved the way the Evidence Act — now the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 — requires, with the certificate for electronic records; privately obtained material raises admissibility and privacy questions that should be anticipated rather than discovered at trial. Bald suspicion, anonymous letters and the testimony of chance witnesses rarely carry a petition alone.</p>

<h2>Defending the allegation</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Test the pleading:</strong> the petition must allege the act with such particulars of time, place and person as the petitioner can reasonably give; a roving allegation invites dismissal and can itself amount to cruelty if reckless.</li>
  <li><strong>Condonation:</strong> resumption of cohabitation with knowledge of the matrimonial offence condones it; Section 23(1)(b) HMA obliges the court to examine condonation even if not pleaded.</li>
  <li><strong>Counter-consequences:</strong> an unproved allegation of adultery, persisted in at trial, is itself recognised as grave mental cruelty and has founded decrees in favour of the accused spouse.</li>
  <li><strong>Mind the co-respondent rule:</strong> objections to non-impleadment of the named paramour should be raised early, not after evidence.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Since 2018, the adultery ground is litigated free of the shadow of criminal complaint leverage — which has, if anything, made pleadings more honest. Petitioners should plead only what they can support; respondents should remember that a false denial collapses faster than a candid case on condonation or connivance.</p>
</div>
